;; Device registry — copy to devices.edn and edit. Read once at startup, so restart
;; the service after changing it by hand. Override the path with $DEVICES_FILE.
;;
;; Writing this file by hand is the fallback, not the usual route. Point a display at the
;; server and it provisions itself: it is issued an :id and a :token on its first request,
;; prints that id on its own screen, and appears on / under "Waiting to be configured".
;; Clicking it opens a form asking only what to call it and where it is, and that writes the
;; entry here. The display starts showing forecasts within seconds — no restart, and no
;; token to invent. The same pages can move or rename it later.
;;
;; Two consequences: the server rewrites this file in full when it does, so comments added
;; below don't survive, and on a live server this file — not the copy on your laptop — is
;; the authoritative one.
;;
;; Keyed by the device's MAC address, which the TRMNL firmware sends as the `ID`
;; header on every request. Case doesn't matter.
;;
;;   :id     required, [a-z0-9-]+, unique. The display's permanent identity: it's the
;;           URL segment (/images/<id>/…, /devices/<id>/…) and the directory name
;;           (archive/<id>/, logs/<id>/), so it's validated strictly at load. Pick one
;;           and never change it — changing an :id moves that display's archive and
;;           telemetry directories and breaks every existing link to it. Rename the
;;           :name instead; that's what it's for. A display that provisions itself is
;;           given one derived from its MAC (e.g. "k9jx8v"), which is the id it prints on
;;           its own screen — so don't rewrite those. Writing this file by hand, pick
;;           whatever you like: a readable id is nicer in `ls archive/`, it just has to be
;;           one you will never want to change.
;;   :name   required. The label shown on the web pages, and nothing else — free-form,
;;           so "Hallway" or "Pappas kök" are both fine. Change it whenever you like.
;;   :lat    required, decimal degrees.
;;   :lon    required, decimal degrees.
;;   :token  required. Handed to the device by /api/setup as its api_key, and checked
;;           against the Access-Token header on every later request. Issued once, at the
;;           display's first contact, and never rotated — the firmware only asks for a
;;           token when it has none, so a display given a second one can never learn it and
;;           is locked out until its credentials are wiped. Changing this by hand has
;;           exactly that effect. Any long random string will do for a new entry:
;;           `head -c 24 /dev/urandom | base64`
;;   :hours  optional. How many hourly points to render; falls back to
;;           $FORECAST_HOURS, then to core/default-forecast-hours (23).

{"AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF" {:id    "hallway"
                      :name  "Hallway"
                      :lat   57.715722
                      :lon   12.035778
                      :token "replace-me-with-something-random"}

 "11:22:33:44:55:66" {:id    "pappa"
                      :name  "Pappa"
                      :lat   57.6667
                      :lon   12.85
                      :token "replace-me-too"
                      :hours 23}}
